# EpiGraph Explorer: a site block of its OWN in the VM Caddyfile, for a
# dedicated Explorer host served at the root. It is not a stanza inside the
# API's site block.
# Assumes the explorer listens on 127.0.0.1:8096 on the same host: either the
# systemd unit (epigraph-explorer.service.example) or a --network host
# container. The binary binds loopback only, so Caddy is the public edge.
#
# WHY ITS OWN HOST. Co-hosting the Explorer under a path of the API's site
# (`handle_path /explorer*`) puts it in one origin with the API's HTML pages
# (the OAuth consent page, and any passkey ceremony page the API serves).
# Then an HTML-injection bug on either side reaches the other, and the
# Explorer's Origin check on POST /auth/logout and /auth/redeem accepts any
# page the API serves. A cookie Path is not an isolation boundary inside one
# origin. On its own host at the root, the Explorer's cookies carry the
# `__Host-` prefix (Secure, Path=/, no Domain), so no other host, a sibling
# under the same parent domain included, can set or shadow them. That closes
# login CSRF by cookie tossing even when the parent domain is not a public
# suffix. Under a base path the prefix is impossible: the explorer then uses
# plain cookie names and logs "base path set: `__Host-` cookie prefix
# unavailable" at startup. A deploy must not show that line.
#
# THE PUBLIC BASE URL HAS AN EMPTY PATH:
#   EPIGRAPH_EXPLORER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://explorer.example.com
# Every link, redirect, og:url and the OAuth redirect_uri
# (`https://explorer.example.com/auth/callback`, which must match the
# oauth_clients row byte for byte) are generated from it. No prefix is
# stripped, so the explorer sees the request path unchanged.
#
# REMOVE ANY OLD CO-HOSTED STANZA. If the API's site block still has a
# `handle_path /explorer* { ... }` or `handle /explorer* { ... }` from an
# earlier deploy, delete it. Serving the Explorer on both origins keeps the
# co-hosting exposure alive.
#
# /oauth/* STAYS ON THE API'S HOST, NOT HERE. Sign-in is the API's own OAuth
# authorization server, and the browser navigates to it directly:
#   GET  /oauth/authorize            (explorer -> API, with PKCE)
#   GET  /oauth/callback             (Google -> API)
#   POST /oauth/authorize/consent    (the consent form's root-relative action)
# These live at the root of the API's EPIGRAPH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL and are
# already exposed for the claude.ai connector. Point EPIGRAPH_OAUTH_BASE_URL
# at that browser-facing API origin. Do not proxy /oauth/* or /api/v1/* on
# the Explorer's host: the explorer reaches the API over loopback via
# EPIGRAPH_API_URL, and /api/v1 can stay private. The explorer's own sign-in
# routes are /auth/*, distinct from the API's /oauth/*.
#
# PASSKEYS. If the API serves WebAuthn (passkey) ceremonies, its relying-party
# id must be the API's exact host, never a registrable parent domain that the
# Explorer's host also sits under. Otherwise a script on the Explorer's origin
# could run a ceremony for the API's credentials.
#
# RATE LIMITING /auth/login. Every anonymous GET of that path stores a
# pending login in the explorer's process-local map (10-minute TTL, 10 000
# entries). The explorer keeps the map bounded by evicting its oldest entries
# rather than refusing, so a flood cannot lock anyone out of sign-in, but it
# can still evict other people's in-flight attempts, which makes them retry.
# An edge rate limit on that one path removes even that. Caddy has no rate
# limiter in the standard build; with the `caddy-ratelimit` module built in,
# add inside the site block:
#
#   @login path /auth/login
#   rate_limit @login {
#       zone explorer_login {
#           key    {remote_host}
#           events 30
#           window 1m
#       }
#   }
#
# Do not rate-limit `/auth/callback`: one slow sign-in behind a shared NAT
# would then fail at the last step.
#
# HEADERS. The explorer sets its own Content-Security-Policy, including
# `frame-ancestors` (the Notion embed depends on it), X-Content-Type-Options,
# Referrer-Policy and, on every response without its own cache policy,
# `Cache-Control: private, no-store` with `Vary: Cookie`. Do not add
# X-Frame-Options or a second CSP in Caddy: a site-wide
# `X-Frame-Options: DENY` breaks the embed. Do not strip the Origin request
# header (Caddy forwards it by default): POST /auth/logout and POST
# /auth/redeem compare it with the public origin.

explorer.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8096
}
